  <h4 data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Key Discussion Topics</h4><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK">Our learning focused on several key areas of the NC Child Welfare Manual, including:</p><ul data-rte-list="default"><li><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK"><strong>Monthly Permanency Planning Contact Record </strong><a target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" class="O1npr zU7jc" href="https://pathtopermanency.org/so/d7Pbt_Cku/c?w=upaGXUtqn7LVXKdbGf5nblW4teRaNJs2mfEY9fuyNVc.eyJ1IjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9wb2xpY2llcy5uY2RoaHMuZ292L3dwLWNvbnRlbnQvdXBsb2Fkcy9DV1MtMTgtMjAyMWEyLnBkZiIsInIiOiJiMjgxYmE0Mi1lMjk5LTRhMWMtYjJhNC05MzE2MTY2MTljMjAiLCJtIjoibWFpbCIsImMiOiIxNDY1MDczYy1mYTNmLTQ1MWYtODAyOS03NWM2YjQ0ZDJjMmMifQ"><strong><u>(DSS 5295</u></strong></a><strong>)</strong><br>We discussed the purpose of this form that is completed by the child’s social worker during monthly home visits and shared feedback on how receiving a copy of this record would be helpful for communication and establishing a positive record of placement, in the event of a social worker threatening to remove a child. A state DSS representative confirmed that this was best practice to provide to placement providers following each monthly home visit; however, no kinship/foster parent present had ever received a copy.</p></li><li><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK"><strong>Child Family Team (CFT) &amp; Permanency Planning Review (PPR) Meetings</strong></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK">We explored the requirement that PPR’s are to be held within the first 60 days of a case and every 90 days thereafter, must include the placement provider (among others), document signatures of attendees and distribute post-meeting notes to attendees. Caregivers insisted that PPR’s are not happening regularly in their cases and very few of those present had ever been included in a PPR.&nbsp;</p></li><li><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK"><strong>Concurrent Planning &amp; Safety Concerns</strong></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK">We talked about advocating for timely permanency and addressing safety concerns. We shared experiences on how concurrent planning has worked in cases where reunification is no longer the primary goal and how safety issues have been handled poorly. FFA and DHHS clarified that reunification is a possibility up until the TPR has been signed, so the ambiguity that many caregivers reported experiencing is understandable, although not ideal.</p></li></ul><h4 data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Moving Forward&nbsp;</h4><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK"> Based on the topics discussed, <strong>the group voted that the most impactful tool for shortening paths to permanency for Mecklenburg County foster children would be for YFS to hold PPR’s in accordance with the state manual’s requirements. </strong>The results of this vote, along with the feedback from all small group discussions, have been shared with DHHS and FFA to help develop an action plan. P2P has a meeting scheduled for October 6 with YFS leadership and county commissioners to address the topic of CFTs.</p>


  










  
  <h4 data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Available Resources&nbsp;</h4><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK"> During the Town Hall, we shared a number of digital resources that are now available on our <a target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" class="O1npr zU7jc" href="https://pathtopermanency.org/so/d7Pbt_Cku/c?w=Gll4WWFM5cmH6sjK_bvqCu04-IWrtQ39j4HW_fRT9N4.eyJ1IjoiaHR0cHM6Ly93d3cucGF0aHRvcGVybWFuZW5jeS5vcmcvcmVzb3VyY2VzIiwiciI6ImExYzY4OTI0LWM4YzgtNDI4ZC1hZWIzLWE4YmVhYzFjNzhkNiIsIm0iOiJtYWlsIiwiYyI6IjE0NjUwNzNjLWZhM2YtNDUxZi04MDI5LTc1YzZiNDRkMmMyYyJ9"><u>website</u></a>. These include:</p><ul data-rte-list="default"><li><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK">Monthly Permanency Planning Contact Record</p></li><li><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK">PPR Form</p></li><li><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK">DSS State Permanency Planning Manual</p></li><li><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK">Case Timeline Tracker</p></li><li><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK">Guide on How to Handle YFS Concerns</p></li></ul>]]></content:encoded><media:content type="image/png" url="https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/6a947e1fc462510da6513955/4e0f501d-f11c-423e-aa07-6baa55c76665/town-hall.png?format=1500w" medium="image" isDefault="true" width="454" height="341"><media:title type="plain">Town Hall Recap &amp; Next Steps for Path to Permanency</media:title></media:content></item><item><title>One Child’s Story Sparks a Movement&#x2014;Will You Help Carry It Forward?</title><dc:creator>Josclyn Reed</dc:creator><pubDate>Mon, 28 Jul 2025 15:13:10 +0000</pubDate><link>https://flute-flugelhorn-43ff.squarespace.com/news/one-childs-story-sparks-a-movement</link><guid isPermaLink="false">6a947e1fc462510da6513955:6ab2dfe5f881742c21b6563d:6ab2dfe5f881742c21b6563e</guid><description><![CDATA[Path to Permanency was inspired by love for that little girl—and a deep 
belief that no child in foster care should ever wait years for a forever 
home. Watch this short video to learn more.]]></description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="">She was full of love. Laughter. Strength.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK">And though she lost her battle with cancer before receiving the permanency she deserved, her story lives on in everything we do.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK"><strong>Path to Permanency</strong>&nbsp;was inspired by love for that little girl—and a deep belief that&nbsp;<strong>no child in foster care should ever wait years for a forever home.</strong></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK">That’s why we’re building a movement to reform the foster care system in Mecklenburg County. We’re using the power of&nbsp;<em>people organizing</em>— bringing together foster parents, social workers, GALs, advocates, elected leaders, and those with lived experience—to hold systems accountable and fight for every child’s right to timely permanency.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="_13-wL _2jbIK">Watch this short video to learn more:</p>


  










  





















  











  
    
      
    
  
  
    
      
        
          
        
      
    
    
    



  








  
  
  




  
  
  
  
    
  


  
